And then he brought me in for a meeting, and I thought I was going to do an episode or two.

511
00:22:42.380 --> 00:22:49.220
And then I ended up just being a recurring character on Star Trek Discovery.

512
00:22:49.480 --> 00:22:53.540
But after the first episode, I really struggled with my lines.

513
00:22:53.760 --> 00:23:01.520
And I called Alex and I said, I know you said you wanted to use me as much as you can.

514
00:23:01.520 --> 00:23:07.260
And I, in my mind, I thought they'll probably use me once or twice and I'll go away.

515
00:23:07.360 --> 00:23:12.520
But I said, I want you to know I struggled really bad.

516
00:23:12.720 --> 00:23:17.100
And if you don't want to use me as much as you want.

517
00:23:17.180 --> 00:23:17.230
It's okay.

518
00:23:17.400 --> 00:23:22.920
I was, yeah, I was like, truly don't feel like you have to hold yourself to that.

519
00:23:22.920 --> 00:23:28.640
And he said, if you're having trouble with the lines, I'll just make them even harder.

520
00:23:28.940 --> 00:23:29.540
Oh, no.

521
00:23:29.700 --> 00:23:30.020
What?

522
00:23:30.300 --> 00:23:30.560
No.

523
00:23:30.820 --> 00:23:33.180
You just, you know, a friend giving me a hard time.

524
00:23:33.180 --> 00:23:33.920
Just to mess with you, yeah.

525
00:23:34.120 --> 00:23:38.380
And he's like, and he also said, and your character will never die.

526
00:23:38.380 --> 00:23:40.840
So that's not around the corner.

527
00:23:41.180 --> 00:23:42.200
So you're in.

528
00:23:42.400 --> 00:23:42.660
Buckle in.

529
00:23:42.660 --> 00:23:43.000
Yeah.

530
00:23:43.300 --> 00:23:44.060
Oh, that's so great.

531
00:23:44.240 --> 00:23:44.460
Yeah.

532
00:23:44.740 --> 00:23:48.780
But now it's so fun to be in the Star Trek world.

533
00:23:48.960 --> 00:23:49.600
My gosh.

534
00:23:49.900 --> 00:23:53.400
I have to ask you about Holly Hunter because I just love her.

535
00:23:53.700 --> 00:23:57.820
You know, I'll never forget when she was on Inside the Actor's Studio.

536
00:23:58.100 --> 00:24:00.340
You know, they ask everyone their favorite curse word.

537
00:24:00.440 --> 00:24:02.880
And people just sort of say s*** or ass s***.

538
00:24:03.140 --> 00:24:03.200
Yeah.

539
00:24:03.640 --> 00:24:06.500
And she just looked right at him and said, c*** sucker.

540
00:24:06.980 --> 00:24:08.700
And I was like, what?

541
00:24:09.140 --> 00:24:10.180
That is brilliant.

542
00:24:10.720 --> 00:24:15.800
And I don't even really know that I really heard anyone say that, like out and about in the world.

543
00:24:15.980 --> 00:24:17.940
You have not heard people say that?

544
00:24:18.060 --> 00:24:19.020
At that time, I was young.

545
00:24:19.160 --> 00:24:19.360
Oh.

546
00:24:19.560 --> 00:24:21.500
And I just remember being like, oh, yeah.

547
00:24:22.060 --> 00:24:23.200
I don't know.

548
00:24:23.440 --> 00:24:23.580
What?

549
00:24:23.840 --> 00:24:25.200
Maybe early 20s.

550
00:24:25.560 --> 00:24:27.940
And you had not heard anyone say that?

551
00:24:27.940 --> 00:24:28.740
We're not buying it.

552
00:24:28.960 --> 00:24:29.760
No, this seeMs.

553
00:24:29.760 --> 00:24:30.740
I was not familiar.

554
00:24:31.160 --> 00:24:32.400
I mean, it was like.

555
00:24:32.400 --> 00:24:33.740
Holly's not going to buy that.

556
00:24:34.960 --> 00:24:35.980
No, for real.

557
00:24:36.100 --> 00:24:41.140
Like, I remember being like, I had heard a lot of like curse words, but I was like, c*** sucker.

558
00:24:41.440 --> 00:24:43.020
I don't think of that one.

559
00:24:43.360 --> 00:24:45.600
That's like, that's fantastic.

560
00:24:46.140 --> 00:24:48.320
It is a unique favorite curse word.

561
00:24:48.320 --> 00:24:48.560
Yes.

562
00:24:48.560 --> 00:24:49.360
I'll give you that.

563
00:24:49.640 --> 00:24:50.120
Anyway.

564
00:24:50.480 --> 00:24:53.040
This is, you guys mentioned nerding out.

565
00:24:53.200 --> 00:24:56.540
And this feels kind of nerdy about the way you're discussing that word.

566
00:24:56.760 --> 00:24:57.000
Oh.

567
00:24:57.280 --> 00:24:57.440
Yeah.

568
00:24:57.740 --> 00:24:58.200
Like.

569
00:24:59.080 --> 00:25:02.120
Like you saying it, like being like,

570
00:25:02.120 --> 00:25:03.740
I hadn't really heard that much.

571
00:25:03.860 --> 00:25:05.940
And you saying, yeah, whatever you were just saying.

572
00:25:06.140 --> 00:25:09.520
It just felt very nerdy around and unnerdy work.

573
00:25:10.000 --> 00:25:10.160
I know.

574
00:25:10.360 --> 00:25:13.220
Well, anyway, I just remember being so surprised by it.

575
00:25:13.280 --> 00:25:14.660
And now you get to work with her.

576
00:25:14.820 --> 00:25:20.960
And I'm worried that someday if I meet her, that I would have that moment where you just go, blah, blah.

577
00:25:21.140 --> 00:25:24.140
And I just say something like, I loved it when you said c*** sucker.

578
00:25:24.400 --> 00:25:26.280
She's going to be like, what are you talking about?

579
00:25:26.280 --> 00:25:29.040
No, she would probably hug you for like two minutes.

580
00:25:29.400 --> 00:25:30.780
She's very affectionate, too.

581
00:25:31.200 --> 00:25:31.500
Aw.

582
00:25:31.840 --> 00:25:32.000
Yeah.

583
00:25:32.000 --> 00:25:33.820
I mean, she's the commander, right?

584
00:25:33.880 --> 00:25:34.520
She's the captain.

585
00:25:34.820 --> 00:25:35.140
Yeah.

586
00:25:35.300 --> 00:25:36.840
She is the top dog.

587
00:25:37.080 --> 00:25:41.340
And she is, man, she's just incredible.

588
00:25:41.340 --> 00:25:44.380
And she's also so fun.

589
00:25:44.960 --> 00:25:48.380
Like, before I left Toronto, we had gone out for lunch.

590
00:25:48.600 --> 00:25:49.960
That went five hours.

591
00:25:50.280 --> 00:25:50.700
No.

592
00:25:50.700 --> 00:25:51.240
Yes.

593
00:25:51.480 --> 00:25:51.960
Yeah.

594
00:25:52.380 --> 00:25:52.680
Yeah.

595
00:25:52.880 --> 00:25:53.760
That's so cool.

596
00:25:53.800 --> 00:25:56.000
I mean, she's an incredible actor.

597
00:25:56.000 --> 00:25:57.560
And she's a serious actor.

598
00:25:57.560 --> 00:26:00.000
And she's also so fun.

599
00:26:00.140 --> 00:26:02.860
And so fun to laugh with, you know?

600
00:26:03.020 --> 00:26:03.580
Oh, I bet.

601
00:26:03.620 --> 00:26:04.220
And talk to.

602
00:26:04.280 --> 00:26:08.520
And she's one of the most curious people I think I've ever met.

603
00:26:08.820 --> 00:26:12.580
And this, I'm always like, oh, this sounds so weird to say.

604
00:26:12.700 --> 00:26:15.420
Because, of course, you should be nice to everyone.

605
00:26:15.420 --> 00:26:22.940
But that's not always the case on sets, especially with somebody as well-known and celebrated.

606
00:26:22.940 --> 00:26:33.000
But she knows the entire crew's name, their jobs, speaks to everybody, sits down like two

607
00:26:33.000 --> 00:26:36.760
inches from their face, so interested in their lives.

608
00:26:36.760 --> 00:26:38.420
Oh, that makes my heart so happy.

609
00:26:38.620 --> 00:26:38.900
Yeah.

610
00:26:39.120 --> 00:26:39.480
Yeah.

611
00:26:39.600 --> 00:26:44.460
It's really a nice thing to come across in this industry.

612
00:26:44.460 --> 00:26:44.510
Sorry.

613
00:26:45.900 --> 00:26:46.300
So.

614
00:26:52.140 --> 00:26:53.340
We'll cut our whispers.

615
00:26:53.720 --> 00:26:55.820
Why would you cut the pivot whispers?

616
00:26:56.060 --> 00:26:56.720
Pivot whispers.

617
00:26:56.800 --> 00:26:57.080
I don't.

618
00:26:57.260 --> 00:26:58.940
You've got to keep it pivoting.

619
00:26:59.040 --> 00:26:59.400
Pivoting.

620
00:26:59.560 --> 00:27:00.320
Oh, I got it.

621
00:27:00.440 --> 00:27:00.960
I got it.

622
00:27:00.960 --> 00:27:01.420
You got it.

623
00:27:01.460 --> 00:27:02.040
You found it.

624
00:27:02.180 --> 00:27:03.100
I found what I wanted.

625
00:27:03.340 --> 00:27:04.360
Is it the watch?

626
00:27:04.840 --> 00:27:06.160
We're not at the watch yet.

627
00:27:06.660 --> 00:27:10.800
Everybody, we're going to do a question about Tig's watch, but we're not there yet.

628
00:27:10.880 --> 00:27:11.100
Okay.

629
00:27:11.360 --> 00:27:11.500
Okay.

630
00:27:11.540 --> 00:27:12.020
Stay tuned.

631
00:27:13.260 --> 00:27:15.000
Should I go to my full voice again?

632
00:27:15.300 --> 00:27:15.700
Yes.

633
00:27:15.940 --> 00:27:16.160
Okay.

634
00:27:16.160 --> 00:27:17.180
Wait, you found your pivot.

635
00:27:17.220 --> 00:27:18.140
I'm not going to.

636
00:27:18.240 --> 00:27:19.100
Stay there.

637
00:27:19.180 --> 00:27:19.960
I'm going to stay there.

638
00:27:20.200 --> 00:27:20.360
Yeah.

639
00:27:20.720 --> 00:27:21.740
I'm going to have a cup of tea.

640
00:27:22.020 --> 00:27:22.220
Okay.

641
00:27:22.360 --> 00:27:22.520
Okay.

642
00:27:22.860 --> 00:27:28.880
Tig, there is a thing about you that is famous, which is that you talk slow.

643
00:27:29.220 --> 00:27:29.540
Yes.

644
00:27:29.760 --> 00:27:30.160
Yes.

645
00:27:30.380 --> 00:27:35.160
You even did a whole bit where you tried to get an auctioneer to train you to talk faSt.

646
00:27:35.300 --> 00:27:35.480
Yeah.

647
00:27:35.800 --> 00:27:36.240
Very funny.

648
00:27:36.940 --> 00:27:37.220
Thank you.

649
00:27:37.880 --> 00:27:44.440
We reached out to the director of your episode, Test the Store, Brent Forrester, and he sent

650
00:27:44.440 --> 00:27:47.640
in an audio clip about what it was like directing you in the episode.

651
00:27:48.360 --> 00:27:49.720
And I want you to have a listen.

652
00:27:50.140 --> 00:27:51.180
I'm sorry.

653
00:27:51.560 --> 00:27:53.020
Should we have given you a heads up?

654
00:27:53.700 --> 00:27:54.740
It's hilarious.

655
00:27:55.400 --> 00:27:56.660
Oh my gosh.

656
00:27:56.960 --> 00:27:59.000
You probably can see where this is going.

657
00:28:00.080 --> 00:28:04.840
For the part of the little girl's mom, Mindy always pictured Tig Notaro.

658
00:28:05.020 --> 00:28:05.920
We didn't audition anybody.

659
00:28:05.920 --> 00:28:08.320
We just offered it to Tig, and she was great.

660
00:28:08.540 --> 00:28:12.380
But at the time, I was concerned about the pacing of the scene.

661
00:28:12.380 --> 00:28:17.240
And after the first take, I walked up to Tig, and I said, hey, Tig, I have a thought.

662
00:28:17.320 --> 00:28:21.400
And she said, you want me to talk faster?

663
00:28:21.880 --> 00:28:23.460
And I said, yeah, how do you know?

664
00:28:23.640 --> 00:28:31.080
And she said, every time they put me on camera, they tell me to talk faster.

665
00:28:31.500 --> 00:28:33.320
And I said, can you talk faster?

666
00:28:33.480 --> 00:28:36.960
And she said, no, I can't.

667
00:28:38.620 --> 00:28:40.180
Do you remember the 6G?

668
00:28:40.180 --> 00:28:44.480
I feel like it is an exchange I have on every set.

669
00:28:45.280 --> 00:28:46.400
Every set.

670
00:28:46.940 --> 00:28:56.000
In fact, it's gotten to the point where when a director comes towards me, I'll put my hand out and say,

671
00:28:56.120 --> 00:28:59.320
before you give me any notes, just know I have no range.

672
00:28:59.680 --> 00:29:00.800
But go on.

673
00:29:01.140 --> 00:29:02.480
What were you going to say?

674
00:29:03.100 --> 00:29:08.700
I just, but it's always getting to that of like, okay, now let's do it faster.

675
00:29:08.700 --> 00:29:14.020
And, you know, and I try, but, you know.

676
00:29:14.300 --> 00:29:17.960
I just think the beauty in your comedy is your pacing.

677
00:29:18.280 --> 00:29:19.180
I just love it.

678
00:29:19.380 --> 00:29:25.440
And I feel like I couldn't imagine you tell a story not the way you tell it.

679
00:29:25.440 --> 00:29:26.000
Yeah.

680
00:29:26.720 --> 00:29:35.020
There, it's, it's, it's, you know, there's a lot of talk about how, just the connection between music and comedy.

681
00:29:36.000 --> 00:29:38.640
And a lot of musicians want to be comedians.

682
00:29:38.760 --> 00:29:40.160
A lot of comedians want to be musicians.

683
00:29:40.180 --> 00:29:44.960
And so much of that, I don't know, the timing.

684
00:29:44.960 --> 00:29:49.220
Like, it feels like a rhythm and a song to me.

685
00:29:49.440 --> 00:29:57.760
The way, like, I can't, the way I deliver my material, it's, it's not thought out.

686
00:29:57.960 --> 00:30:02.680
It's just the natural beat and rhythm of the story.

687
00:30:02.680 --> 00:30:13.640
And people have asked me, do you decide to pause there for two seconds or do you decide to, and I'm like, no, there's no thought.

688
00:30:13.820 --> 00:30:17.080
It's truly just how I deliver it.

689
00:30:17.600 --> 00:30:19.100
Have you always had that cadence?

690
00:30:19.720 --> 00:30:27.720
Um, I mean, I've always been, you know, told I was, yeah, slow and, and, and dry.

691
00:30:28.080 --> 00:30:37.420
And, um, but I think it's highlighted on stage in front of an audience, but it's not on purpose.

692
00:30:37.760 --> 00:30:38.000
Mm-hmm.

693
00:30:39.100 --> 00:30:45.900
I know just back when I did a ton of improv, you know, you would do a workshop and you would teach these young improvisers.

694
00:30:45.900 --> 00:30:53.960
And I always sort of felt bad saying this because I was like, oh, they're all here to learn, but you can't teach timing.

695
00:30:54.200 --> 00:30:54.520
Mm-hmm.

696
00:30:54.620 --> 00:31:00.480
You can teach a lot of things, I feel like, for comedy, but timing is just something instinctual, I think, personally.

697
00:31:00.720 --> 00:31:01.200
I do too.

698
00:31:01.440 --> 00:31:06.860
And it's, and it's a very different timing on and off stage as well.

699
00:31:07.120 --> 00:31:12.940
I think that it's just highlighted a little more.

700
00:31:13.180 --> 00:31:13.580
Yeah.

701
00:31:14.040 --> 00:31:14.360
Yeah.

702
00:31:15.220 --> 00:31:20.200
We got a fan question from, uh, Natalie L.

703
00:31:20.320 --> 00:31:20.940
in Boston.

704
00:31:21.240 --> 00:31:21.800
Hey, Natalie.

705
00:31:22.600 --> 00:31:23.660
I love you, Tig.

706
00:31:24.220 --> 00:31:28.800
Did you get to practice with the actress who played your daughter prior to the shoot day?

707
00:31:30.670 --> 00:31:33.870
Um, no, I did not.

708
00:31:35.530 --> 00:31:39.290
I, um, I was going to say I wish I had, but I don't even know.

709
00:31:39.290 --> 00:31:40.550
Did you know she was playing your daughter?

710
00:31:40.730 --> 00:31:41.230
No, I'm kidding.

711
00:31:43.390 --> 00:31:46.490
I honestly don't think I knew anything.

712
00:31:46.770 --> 00:31:47.210
Like, who's this person?

713
00:31:47.250 --> 00:31:49.350
Yeah, like, why is she stealing my thunder?

714
00:31:49.350 --> 00:31:50.470
I'm referring to her.

715
00:31:50.690 --> 00:31:51.990
Get out of my scene, kid.

716
00:31:52.750 --> 00:31:53.730
Um, yeah.

717
00:31:54.030 --> 00:31:57.370
No, I didn't run it with anybody.

718
00:31:58.110 --> 00:32:01.050
And is this upsetting to the office fans?

719
00:32:01.210 --> 00:32:03.590
If somebody is like, oh, I hadn't seen it.

720
00:32:04.190 --> 00:32:05.610
Um, you know what I mean?

721
00:32:05.610 --> 00:32:10.330
Like, I know there's rabid fans where they might be offended or something.

722
00:32:10.690 --> 00:32:12.430
Oh, that you hadn't seen it before?

723
00:32:12.570 --> 00:32:12.810
No.

724
00:32:12.930 --> 00:32:13.130
Yeah.

725
00:32:13.150 --> 00:32:15.510
No, because we've had a lot of different guest stars.

726
00:32:15.670 --> 00:32:19.130
Some people were like, I was so nervous because I'd seen every episode.

727
00:32:19.330 --> 00:32:19.490
Yeah.

728
00:32:19.490 --> 00:32:23.550
And then other people were like, I knew it was a big show, but I hadn't seen it yet.

729
00:32:23.890 --> 00:32:23.990
Yeah.

730
00:32:24.130 --> 00:32:24.310
Yeah.

731
00:32:24.510 --> 00:32:27.250
No, and everyone's experience is completely different.

732
00:32:27.470 --> 00:32:28.130
So, no.

733
00:32:28.290 --> 00:32:29.070
You will not offend anybody.

734
00:32:29.070 --> 00:32:29.670
You'll be fine.

735
00:32:29.830 --> 00:32:35.970
But I do, I want to mention again that I have seen many episodes of The Office.

736
00:32:36.190 --> 00:32:36.510
Yes.

737
00:32:36.670 --> 00:32:42.190
Because we were just blown away that our, um, our four-year-olds were just, like, so into it.

738
00:32:42.230 --> 00:32:44.330
Did they have a character that they were most like?

739
00:32:44.430 --> 00:32:45.710
A lot of little kids like Dwight.

740
00:32:45.850 --> 00:32:47.130
Yeah, I was going to guess Dwight.

741
00:32:47.290 --> 00:32:47.490
Yeah.

742
00:32:47.610 --> 00:32:48.150
It was Dwight.

743
00:32:48.310 --> 00:32:48.570
Yeah.

744
00:32:48.570 --> 00:32:48.990
Yeah.

745
00:32:49.310 --> 00:32:50.330
And I know who Dwight is.

746
00:32:50.570 --> 00:32:51.050
Rain.

747
00:32:51.270 --> 00:32:53.790
You're not just, you're not just saying, sure, sure, yeah.

748
00:32:53.950 --> 00:32:54.470
Yeah, yeah, yeah.

749
00:32:54.670 --> 00:32:55.490
No, for sure.

750
00:32:55.650 --> 00:32:56.050
For sure.

